@page "/settings"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false))

@using BgQuiz_Blazor.Client.Quiz
@using XgFilter_Lib.Enums

@inject QuizSettings UserSettings
@inject QuizController Controller
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>BgQuiz — Settings</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-3" style="max-width:800px">
    <h1>Settings</h1>
    @* The lead promises persistence, not visible effect: one setting below
       deliberately shows itself on the next page rather than on this one (the
       fold — see its own fine print), so a blanket "takes effect immediately"
       would be a claim the page then breaks. "Kept as you make it" is the part
       that is true of every one of them, and it is also the part a reader is looking
       for when they wonder where the Save button is. *@
    <p class="lead">
        Every change here is kept as you make it and remembered in this
        browser — there is nothing to save.
    </p>

    @* Gated on hydration so no control paints a default the stored settings are
       about to overwrite. On any ordinary visit the task is already complete and
       this branch is taken on the first render anyway. *@
    @if (_hydrated)
    {
        <fieldset class="mb-4">
            <legend class="h5">The board</legend>

            <fieldset class="mb-3">
                <legend class="form-label mb-1">Your home board is on the</legend>
                <div class="form-check">
                    <input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="homeBoardSide"
                           id="settingsSideRight"
                           checked="@UserSettings.HomeBoardOnRight"
                           @onchange="@(() => SetHomeBoardOnRightAsync(true))" />
                    <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsSideRight">Right</label>
                </div>
                <div class="form-check">
                    <input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="homeBoardSide"
                           id="settingsSideLeft"
                           checked="@(!UserSettings.HomeBoardOnRight)"
                           @onchange="@(() => SetHomeBoardOnRightAsync(false))" />
                    <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsSideLeft">Left</label>
                </div>
            </fieldset>

            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsRandomizeSide"
                       checked="@UserSettings.RandomizeSidePerProblem"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetRandomizeSidePerProblemAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsRandomizeSide">
                    Use a random side for each problem
                </label>
            </div>
            <div class="form-text">
                In real play the side is not yours to choose, and a position you
                have seen before is harder to recognise mirrored. Each problem
                draws its own side, and keeps it while you answer and read the
                solution. The choice above applies whenever this is off.
            </div>

            @* The maximize mode (issue #41), and the ONLY control for it — ruled
               (SPEC-quiz-view.md §3, fork D): no on-page toggle on the quiz
               itself, which would be a second write surface for one fact and
               would force QuizSettings to grow the notify plumbing its contract
               defers until a real second consumer exists.

               The fine print leads with what disappears and closes with the
               consequence the model ratified rather than leaving it to be
               discovered: the board is deliberately a different size while
               answering than while reading, so a user who notices it moving is
               reading the feature working, not a bug. Same posture as the fold
               row below, which states its deferral for the same reason. *@
            <div class="form-check mt-3">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsMaximizeBoard"
                       checked="@UserSettings.MaximizeBoardWhileAnswering"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetMaximizeBoardWhileAnsweringAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsMaximizeBoard">
                    Make the board as large as possible while you answer
                </label>
            </div>
            <div class="form-text">
                While you answer, the score line and the prompt below the board are
                hidden, and the board is drawn without the space the solution's
                analysis panel needs — so it fills the screen. Everything comes
                back when you submit and read the solution, so the board changes
                size between answering and reading.
            </div>
        </fieldset>

        @* The depth treatment (issues halheinrich/backgammon#150 and
           halheinrich/backgammon#66). Its OWN fieldset rather than the board's:
           both settings change the analysis panel the solution view puts beside
           the board, and neither touches the board itself. The page groups by
           the thing that moves — which is the whole reason the maximize row's
           placement is worth pinning — so filing these under "The board" would
           cost that grouping its meaning.

           Two controls and not one grouped control: they are independent
           (either, both, or neither is a real choice) and nothing composes them.

           The ceiling is a dropdown over the producer's own level ladder (ruled
           2026-08-28/29), not the checkbox it shipped as. A checkbox could not
           offer "show only rollouts" — the top of the ladder — which is the
           selection the ask was about, and it forced the words to carry a level
           the control could not say. The options come from QuizSettings, which
           enumerates them from the producer's enum in its contractual order;
           this page states no level of its own. *@
        <fieldset class="mb-4">
            <legend class="h5">The analysis panel</legend>

            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsDepthFirst"
                       checked="@UserSettings.SortAnalysisByDepthFirst"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetSortAnalysisByDepthFirstAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsDepthFirst">
                    Sort the analysis by depth first
                </label>
            </div>
            @* Leads with what changes, then rules out the two misreadings the
               change invites: that equity order is gone (it still breaks ties),
               and that the numbers move with the rows (they do not — a play
               keeps its own rank and markers wherever it lands). *@
            <div class="form-text">
                In the solution, the plays are listed most deeply analyzed first
                instead of best equity first — so a play you rolled out sits at the
                top rather than wherever its equity leaves it. Plays analyzed to the
                same depth keep their equity order, and every play keeps its own
                rank number and markers as it moves.
            </div>

            @* The empty option is the default and says what it does ("Hide
               nothing") rather than sitting blank: an unselected dropdown is
               otherwise indistinguishable from one whose value failed to load.
               Its value is the empty token, which is what LevelFromToken reads
               back as null — the page never spells the null itself. *@
            <div class="mt-3">
                <label class="form-label mb-1" for="settingsHiddenLevel">
                    Hide plays analyzed at this level and below
                </label>
                @* The wrapper is the ruler and the select reads it back at 115%
                   (halheinrich/backgammon#170) — the two classes are one
                   mechanism and app.css carries the whole of the reasoning. It
                   is here rather than as a width beside the markup because the
                   options are the producer's, listed by HideableLevels and
                   worded by ToLabel(), so this page cannot know how wide they
                   are; it can only ask for them to be measured. *@
                <div class="hidden-level-field">
                    <select class="form-select hidden-level-select" id="settingsHiddenLevel"
                            value="@QuizSettings.ToLevelToken(UserSettings.MaximumHiddenCandidateAnalysisLevel)"
                            @onchange="@(e => SetMaximumHiddenLevelAsync(e.Value as string))">
                        <option value="">Hide nothing</option>
                        @foreach (var level in QuizSettings.HideableLevels)
                        {
                            <option value="@QuizSettings.ToLevelToken(level)">@level.ToLabel()</option>
                        }
                    </select>
                </div>
            </div>
            @* Leads with the reach of the choice, because "and below" is the one
               thing a ladder of levels does not show: the list is a rigor order
               in which the ply and XG Roller families interleave, so 4-ply also
               takes XG Roller with it. The two ends are named because they are
               the selections a reader is least sure of.

               The exemptions are stated, not left to be discovered: a setting
               whose name is "hide" has to say what it will never hide, or a user
               cannot tell a thinned list from a list that lost the row they were
               looking for. They are the producer's contract, which is why the
               words can promise them — and they hold at the top of the ladder
               too, which is what makes "show only rollouts" safe to offer. *@
            <div class="form-text">
                The level you pick is hidden along with every level below it, so
                4-ply also hides XG Roller, 3-ply and everything shallower, and
                XG Roller++ leaves only the plays you rolled out. A shallow look
                at a play is not the analysis you came to read, and a
                long list of them buries the analysis that is. The best play and your
                own answer are always listed whatever their depth, along with the
                play that was actually made; rolled-out plays are always listed too,
                and so is any play whose depth was never recorded.
            </div>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend class="h5">The navigation panel</legend>
            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsKeepNavFolded"
                       checked="@UserSettings.KeepNavigationPanelFolded"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetKeepNavigationPanelFoldedAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsKeepNavFolded">
                    Keep the navigation panel folded
                </label>
            </div>
            @* The deferral is stated, not left to be discovered. Turning this on
               and seeing nothing happen would otherwise read as the setting
               failing — a reading the checkbox alone cannot rule out. The words
               carry the reason too ("how a page starts out"), because that is
               what makes the delay obviously deliberate rather than slow. *@
            <div class="form-text">
                The blue panel down the left of a wide screen starts folded away on
                every page you open, giving its width back to the board. You can
                still open it any time with the strip at the left edge; this only
                decides how a page starts out, so it takes hold as you move on
                from here rather than folding the panel you are using now.
            </div>
        </fieldset>
    }

    @* The way back to a running quiz. Copied from Help — same predicate, same
       markup, same words — rather than designed: the two pages are in the same
       position (reachable from any state, so never redirecting the way Stats
       does) and a user who has met one affordance should meet the same one
       here. Outside the hydration gate on purpose: returning to the quiz has
       nothing to do with which settings have loaded.

       It lives on the page and not in the nav panel because the panel renders
       statically and cannot know a quiz is live — the same constraint that put
       the fold applier in JS. *@
    @if (Controller.HasStarted && !Controller.IsFinished)
    {
        <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="BackToQuiz">
            Back to quiz
        </button>
    }
</div>
